<%# The flagship page. It has one job: convert a claim a visitor has heard
    from every app they've ever used into something specific enough to
    check. Every paragraph should name a mechanism, not a value. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Almost every assistant works the same way underneath: you type something,
    it travels to a company's servers, a model there reads it, and an answer
    comes back. Whatever the privacy policy says, the arrangement is that
    your medical questions, your finances and your family are somebody else's
    inbound traffic.
  </p>
  <p>
    This one is built the other way round. The app runs on a computer in your
    home, and the model it thinks with can run on a computer in your home
    too. There is no account to make, no server of ours to reach, and no
    version of this software that works differently if you pay more.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What that means, concretely</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The model is wherever you point it") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The app talks to one model, and you choose which. Point it at one
        running in your house — the same machine, or another one down the
        hall — and you can unplug the internet and everything on these pages
        still works. The big hosted models are on the menu too, for a
        household that wants them, and one switch in Settings takes them off.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Your data is files on your disk") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        One database file and one folder of attachments. You can back them
        up, copy them to another machine, or delete them, without asking
        anyone. Nothing is stored in a format only we can read.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The house doesn't read your mail") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Each person's things are private to them by default — not "private
        from the internet", private from the other people using the same
        installation. Sharing is per item or per area, always something you
        switched on, and always visible to you.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Some things are sealed even from the software") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The password vault and the diary are encrypted with a key derived
        from your own login password. Somebody with the database file and full
        access to the machine still cannot read either one. That is also true
        of you, if you lose both the password and the recovery code you were
        given — which is the honest cost of it being real.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The parts that do touch the internet</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Some features reach outside the house, because they cannot do their job
    otherwise. Each is off until you set it up, and each is listed here
    rather than buried.
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">A hosted model, if you choose one</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        If you pick one of the big providers instead of a model at home, what
        the app asks it — which can include the document it is reading for
        you — goes to that company, under their terms. Settings makes the case
        for staying home before it offers them, and one switch takes them off
        the list entirely.
        <%= link_to "Running your own →", about_page_path("models"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">Email, if you connect a mailbox</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        The app connects to your mail provider to read your mail down onto
        your own disk. It talks to your provider and to nobody else, and it
        never sends anything on your behalf without you pressing send.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">Feeds, if you subscribe to any</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        The reader fetches the sites you subscribed to, on a polite schedule,
        the same way any feed reader does. Those sites learn that somebody at
        your address is subscribed — which is what subscribing is.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">Calendars and contacts, if you link them</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        A linked calendar, address book, or Meetup or Eventbrite account
        syncs with that provider and nobody else.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">Local events, if you follow a place</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        The event radar checks the listings you added for a place. The
        big ticketing searches are off by default, and each one says exactly
        what it would send before you turn it on.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">A web page, when you hand it one</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Importing a recipe or reading a job posting fetches that one address,
        once, because you pasted it.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">Alerts on your phone, if you turn them on</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        A text message goes out through whatever you set up to send it, and a
        browser notification through your browser's own push service. The
        message is a line or two, not your files.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">Storage elsewhere, if you configure it</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Photos and files live on your own disk unless whoever installed the
        app points them at a storage service instead.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">Share links, if you make one</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        You can hand somebody outside the house a link to a recipe or an
        album. That link works without an account, which is the point of it,
        so treat it as public and revoke it when you're done.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Including this page</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The pages you are reading are part of the software, and they are the same
    on every installation of it. They describe what the app can do; they
    contain nothing about the household running this copy — not a name, not a
    count, not which parts of it are switched on. If you would rather this
    installation answered strangers with nothing at all, one checkbox in
    Settings turns the whole public side off.
  </p>
</section>
